format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: inside-the-atom

objective u1-unstable-changes
statement: Know that an unstable nucleus can change and give off radiation, and that this happens naturally.
misconception: Radioactivity is something people create, and anything nuclear is dangerous.

item u1-l5-choice-unstable-does
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-up3-ch10

prompt:
    An atom has an unstable nucleus. What can happen to it?

options:
    {"id":"change","label":"The nucleus can change and give off radiation"}
    {"id":"same","label":"Nothing: it stays exactly as it is for ever","note":"Unstable means it can change."}
    {"id":"vanish","label":"The whole atom disappears","note":"The nucleus changes into a different nucleus. Nothing vanishes."}

answer: change

explanation:
    An unstable nucleus can change and give off radiation. That is radioactivity.

item u1-l5-sort-source
type: sort
difficulty: 2
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-potassium-40
references: ref-iaea-isotopes

prompt:
    Where do these radioactive atoms come from? Sort each one.

buckets:
    {"id":"nature","label":"Found in nature","icon":"AqLeaf"}
    {"id":"people","label":"Made by people","icon":"AqFlask"}

items:
    {"id":"k40","label":"Potassium-40 in a banana","bucket":"nature"}
    {"id":"u","label":"Uranium in a rock","bucket":"nature"}
    {"id":"c14","label":"Carbon-14 made high in the air","bucket":"nature"}
    {"id":"hosp","label":"An isotope made in a reactor for a hospital","bucket":"people"}

explanation:
    People can make radioactive atoms, but most radioactivity around us is natural: in rocks, in the air, in food and even in you.

item u1-l5-tom-human-made
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-potassium-40

statement:
    Radioactive atoms only exist because people made them.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. Radioactive atoms occur in nature. Potassium-40 is in bananas and in your own body, and carbon-14 is made high in the air.

item u1-x-potassium
type: true_or_myth
difficulty: 2
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-potassium-40

statement:
    A banana contains a tiny amount of natural radioactive potassium.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. A small share of the potassium in a banana is potassium-40, which is radioactive.

item u1-x-rock-natural
type: choice
difficulty: 2
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-becquerel-1896
references: ref-iaea-isotopes

prompt:
    A rock from the ground gives off a little radiation. How can that be?

options:
    {"id":"natural","label":"Some atoms in it are naturally unstable"}
    {"id":"people","label":"Someone must have made it radioactive","note":"Radioactivity is natural. Many rocks contain unstable atoms."}

answer: natural

explanation:
    Some atoms in the rock, such as uranium, are naturally unstable, so it gives off a little radiation.
